@* One card tile. ShouldRender is overridden because Blazor treats a class-typed parameter as
   always-changed, which would re-render every visible tile on any state change.

   There is no drag handling here: touch's implicit pointer capture makes per-tile pointerenter
   unusable for sweeping. data-idx lets wwwroot/js/gridsweep.js hit-test with elementFromPoint
   instead, and it paints the .sel highlight directly so a sweep costs no Blazor renders.

   The tooltip is text only: a tile is the full card art, so an art preview on hover would show
   what is already on screen, while the tile has no room for the name, set or number. The list
   layout is the opposite case and does preview the art from its thumbnail. *@

@* Right-click removes a copy, the desktop counterpart to left-click adding one, without switching
   the whole grid into remove mode. The browser menu is suppressed on the tile only.

   A comment cannot live inside the attribute list below. Razor does not treat it as a comment
   there — it emits the text as an attribute name, and the browser throws
   "InvalidCharacterError: Element.setAttribute: Invalid attribute name" at render time, taking the
   whole grid down. It compiles, and bUnit's DOM is a parser rather than a browser, so it renders
   the nonsense without complaint; MarkupValidityTests scans every rendered page for names a
   browser would refuse.

   The tile is a cell of the grid its container declares, named by a sentence CardGrid builds so
   the keyboard cursor and the tile describe the card the same way. It carries no tabindex: the
   container holds the only tab stop and moves aria-activedescendant. *@
<div class="card-tile @(Count > 0 ? "owned" : "") @(Picked > 0 ? "picked" : "") @(Gold ? "gold" : "")"
     data-idx="@Index" data-tip="@Tip"
     id="@DomId" role="gridcell" aria-label="@Label"
     @onclick="OnClick"
     @oncontextmenu="RightClick" @oncontextmenu:preventDefault="true"
     @onpointerdown="PointerDown" @onpointerup="PointerUp"
     @onpointercancel="CancelHold" @onpointerleave="CancelHold" @onpointermove="PointerMove">
    @* The art, and nothing on top of it.
       ==================================================================================
       The count, the info button and the heart were all badges over the artwork. One was fine,
       two were busy, three covered three corners of the thing you are looking at in order to
       recognise it -- and the art is the whole reason this layout exists rather than the list.

       So they moved below, into a caption strip, and the art element became its own box: it owns
       the aspect ratio, the rounding, the overflow clip and the gold flair, all of which used to
       belong to the tile and would otherwise squash the art by the height of the caption. *@
    <span class="art @(Gold ? "flair gold-flair" : "")">
        @* No rarity badge. The art carries the rarity mark itself. The list layout does show
           rarity, because there the art is a 32px thumbnail. *@
        <img data-src="@Card.ArtUrl" data-src-alt="@Card.ArtFallbackUrls"
             alt="@Card.Name" decoding="async" draggable="false" />

        @* Shown only while the art is on its way, hidden by CSS the moment it arrives or fails.
           A real element rather than a pseudo-element: gold flair already owns both of the art
           box's, and a sibling selector off the image's own data-state is what makes "loading"
           and "failed" distinguishable without JavaScript touching this at all. *@
        <span class="art-wait" aria-hidden="true"></span>

        @* Art that is never going to arrive. A set is playable in-game days before the community
           CDN has scanned its cards, so on a brand-new set every tile fails at once -- the one
           time the grid is a wall of broken-image markers rather than the odd gap.

           The set and number stand in, because that is the same pair the game prints in the card's
           own corner: it is what you match against when the picture is what you have not got.

           The name goes above them, on the tiles wide enough to read one on. The caption below
           carries it as well, but clipped to one line -- and on a phone the caption is over the
           art and the name is not drawn at all. CSS decides "wide enough" off the tile's own
           width, so the span is rendered either way and simply not shown on a narrow one.

           aria-hidden, like the spinner: the tile's own label names the card, and a screen reader
           reading "Illumise B4a 2" after it would be repeating the label for no one who needed
           it. *@
        <span class="art-none" aria-hidden="true"
              data-set="@Card.Set" data-nr="@Card.Number"><span class="nm">@Card.Name</span></span>

        @* How many you own, back on the art at every width rather than only on a phone.
           ==================================================================================
           The three badges that used to cover the art were a count, an info button and a heart,
           and the note above is right that three was two too many. It does not follow that the
           right number is none: one was fine, and the count is the one, because it is the only
           thing here that is a quantity. A caption row can say a number but it cannot make one
           read at a glance across six columns, and "how many of this do I have" is the question
           the grid is scanned for.

           So it is the only badge, and the caption keeps the three things that are text -- the
           printed number, the name, the rarity -- plus the heart, which is a control rather than
           a fact and sits at the end with the other one.

           Inside the art box rather than over the tile, so its inset is measured from the picture
           it has to stay clear of. On a phone the caption is absolutely positioned and shows
           nothing at all, and this is what is left. *@
        <span class="cnt @(Count > 0 ? "" : "none")">@(Count > 0 ? Count.ToString() : "")</span>

        @* Distinct from the owned count: this is "I just pulled this" rather than "I own this".
           Both appear on one tile on the log screen, which is why they are a pair rather than one
           element with two meanings. *@
        @if (Picked > 0)
        {
            <span class="pick">@(Picked > 1 ? $"+{Picked}" : "+")</span>
        }
    </span>

    <span class="cap">

    @* The number the card is printed with, which is how a set is navigated: the grid is in number
       order by default, and "I am looking for 143" is answerable by eye only if the numbers are on
       screen. It was in the tooltip and in the accessible label and nowhere a sighted user could
       read it without hovering.

       aria-hidden because the tile's own label already names the set and number, and a screen
       reader meeting it twice per tile across three thousand tiles is noise. *@
    <span class="no" aria-hidden="true">@Card.Number</span>

    @* The card's name, and the way into its detail page.
       ==================================================================================
       It said "info" while it was a button over the art, where a name would not have fitted and
       there was nothing to name -- the art was right there. Below the art the same six characters
       are a label for a thing that already has one, and the printed name on a 200px tile is small,
       stylised and sometimes behind an ex badge. So the button became the name, which is both the
       useful text and the same convention the list view uses: the name is the link.

       Detail still needs its own target -- a tap on the tile means "I own one more", so it cannot
       also mean "tell me about this" -- and it is still gone while a sweep is armed, because a link
       across the middle of the tile would swallow the start of a drag. *@
    @if (ShowInfo)
    {
        @* Its context menu is stopped rather than prevented, so "open in new tab" still works on
           the one thing here that is genuinely a link. *@
        @* Out of the tab order: a focus stop per tile would put thousands of them between the grid
           and whatever follows it. The keyboard route to detail is `i` on the cursor, which
           gridkeys.js sends to KeyOpen. *@
        <a class="info" href="card/@Card.Key" title="@Card.Name"
           aria-label="Detail for @Card.Name"
           tabindex="-1"
           @onclick:stopPropagation="true" @onpointerdown:stopPropagation="true"
           @oncontextmenu:stopPropagation="true">@Card.Name</a>
    }

    @* The rarity, last of the three facts and the one that decides whether a gap matters: a
       missing common is tonight's problem and a missing crown is this year's. The glyphs the game
       prints, from the same rungs the list view and the filter chips draw, so a chip and a card
       agree on what a rarity looks like.

       Null where the grid was not given a ladder -- the picker screens pass no RungOf -- and then
       the caption is simply one item shorter rather than carrying an empty slot. Nothing lines up
       across tiles here that a missing rarity would throw out, because it is the last item and
       the name beside it takes the room. *@
    @if (Rung is { } rung)
    {
        <span class="rr glyphs @rung.GlyphClass" aria-hidden="true">@rung.Glyphs</span>
    }

    @* Wanting a card and owning it are different facts, and this is the screen where you notice
       the first one: you are already looking at the set, and the alternative was open the card,
       mark it, come back.

       Its own target, and in the caption rather than over the art -- a tap on the tile means "I
       own one more", so wanting cannot also be a tap on the tile, but it no longer has to cover a
       corner of the picture to say so. *@
    @if (OnWant.HasDelegate)
    {
        <button class="want @(Wanted > 0 ? "on" : "")" type="button"
                @onclick="Want" @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                @onpointerdown:stopPropagation="true"
                @oncontextmenu:stopPropagation="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                aria-pressed="@Aria.Flag(Wanted > 0)"
                aria-label="Want @Card.Name">
            @* Drawn, not typed: the heart characters that carry a filled and an outlined form are
               not in every system font, and the pair that are do not match each other's weight. *@
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="11" height="11" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path d="M8 14S1.6 9.8 1.6 5.9A3.4 3.4 0 0 1 8 4.3a3.4 3.4 0 0 1 6.4 1.6C14.4 9.8 8 14 8 14z"
                      fill="@(Wanted > 0 ? "currentColor" : "none")"
                      stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            </svg>
        </button>
    }
    </span>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public PocketCard Card { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public int Index { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Element id, so the grid's aria-activedescendant can point at this tile.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? DomId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The accessible name. Built by CardGrid so every surface says the same thing.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public int Count { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Copies picked in the current action, e.g. cards pulled from a pack being logged.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Picked { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this card has earned its gold flair. Passed in rather than derived here: the rule
    /// needs the rarity ladder, and the tile knows only its own card.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Gold { get; set; }
    /// <param name="Invert">
    /// Do the opposite of the grid's current mode — shift-click or right-click. The tile does not
    /// know whether that means add or remove; the grid owns the mode.
    /// </param>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<(int Index, bool Invert)> OnActivate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// False while a sweep is armed. Both affordances would fight the sweep: the button would
    /// swallow the start of a drag, and a slow drag would read as a long press.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowInfo { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Raised on a long press. The page navigates; the tile only detects.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<PocketCard> OnHold { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Copies wanted on the chase list the page is pointing at. Zero draws an empty heart.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Wanted { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// This card's rung on the rarity ladder, for the caption's rarity mark. Passed in for the
    /// same reason <see cref="Gold"/> is: the ladder is the grid's, and a tile knows only its own
    /// card. Null on a grid given no ladder, and then the caption omits the mark.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RarityRung? Rung { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Toggle the card on that chase list. Absent where the page has no chase list to write to, which
    /// is what hides the heart — the log-a-pack grid, for one, where a tap means something else
    /// entirely.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<PocketCard> OnWant { get; set; }

    private Task Want() => OnWant.InvokeAsync(Card);

    private PocketCard? _lastCard;
    private int _lastCount = -1;
    private int _lastIndex = -1;
    private int _lastPicked = -1;
    private bool _lastShowInfo = true;
    private bool _lastGold;
    private int _lastWanted = -1;

    protected override bool ShouldRender()
    {
        if (ReferenceEquals(_lastCard, Card) && _lastCount == Count
            && _lastIndex == Index && _lastPicked == Picked && _lastShowInfo == ShowInfo
            && _lastGold == Gold && _lastWanted == Wanted)
            return false;

        _lastCard = Card;
        _lastCount = Count;
        _lastIndex = Index;
        _lastPicked = Picked;
        _lastShowInfo = ShowInfo;
        _lastGold = Gold;
        _lastWanted = Wanted;
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>Caption for the hover art: enough to identify the card without reading the image.</summary>
    private string Tip => $"{Card.Name}\n{Card.Set} {Card.Number} · {Card.Rarity}";

    // ---- long press --------------------------------------------------------------------

    private const int HoldMs = 450;
    private const double MoveTolerance = 10;

    private CancellationTokenSource? _hold;
    private bool _held;
    private double _downX;
    private double _downY;

    private async Task PointerDown(PointerEventArgs e)
    {
        if (!ShowInfo || e.Button != 0) return;

        _held = false;
        _downX = e.ClientX;
        _downY = e.ClientY;

        _hold?.Cancel();
        _hold = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _hold.Token;

        try
        {
            await Task.Delay(HoldMs, token);
        }
        catch (TaskCanceledException)
        {
            return;
        }

        // The tap that follows must not also add a copy, so the flag is checked in OnClick.
        _held = true;
        await OnHold.InvokeAsync(Card);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A finger that travels is scrolling, not holding. Without this, any slow scroll that began
    /// on a tile would open card detail.
    /// </summary>
    private void PointerMove(PointerEventArgs e)
    {
        if (_hold is null || _hold.IsCancellationRequested) return;

        if (Math.Abs(e.ClientX - _downX) > MoveTolerance || Math.Abs(e.ClientY - _downY) > MoveTolerance)
            CancelHold();
    }

    private void PointerUp() => _hold?.Cancel();

    private void CancelHold() => _hold?.Cancel();

    private Task OnClick(MouseEventArgs e)
    {
        // Swallowed exactly once: the click that ends a long press would otherwise increment the
        // count on the way out of navigating away.
        if (_held)
        {
            _held = false;
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        return OnActivate.InvokeAsync((Index, e.ShiftKey));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Right-click means the opposite of a left-click, exactly as shift-click does. It does not
    /// consult <c>_held</c>: a long press is a touch gesture and cannot produce a right-click.
    /// </summary>
    private Task RightClick(MouseEventArgs e) => OnActivate.InvokeAsync((Index, true));
}
